# Important Days and Observances: The Calendar of the GK Paper

> Every general-awareness paper carries at least one "which day is
> observed on ..." question, and every year candidates lose those
> marks to a date they half-remember. The fix is to learn the
> calendar as twelve short clusters, each anchored to a story — a
> birth anniversary, a treaty, a declaration — so that the date and
> the reason arrive together.

## 1. The Winter and Spring Cluster — January to March

| Date | Observance | The anchor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 12 January | National Youth Day | Swami Vivekananda's birthday |
| 15 January | Army Day | K.M. Cariappa taking command in 1949 |
| 25 January | National Voters' Day | Election Commission founded 1950 |
| 26 January | Republic Day | Constitution in force, 1950 |
| 28 February | National Science Day | Raman effect announced, 1928 |
| 8 March | International Women's Day | Labour movements, early 1900s |
| 21 March | International Day of Forests; World Poetry Day | — |
| 22 March | World Water Day | UN, 1993 |

[definition]
**National Science Day (28 February)** marks the day in 1928 when
**C.V. Raman** announced the discovery of the **Raman effect** to
the press; the announcement itself preceded the 1930 Nobel Prize by
two years, which is why the day belongs to the discovery, not the
prize.
[/definition]

The January cluster is anniversary-shaped: Vivekananda (born 12
January 1863) gives the Youth Day its date; Field Marshal Cariappa
became the first Indian Commander-in-Chief on 15 January 1949. When
a paper asks "why is 15 January observed as Army Day," the options
always include a war or a regimental date — the correct anchor is
the Indian command of the Army.

## 2. The High-Season Cluster — April to June

| Date | Observance | The anchor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 5 April | National Maritime Day | *S.S. Loyalty*'s maiden voyage, Scindia Steam Navigation, 1919 |
| 7 April | World Health Day | WHO founded, 1948 |
| 22 April | Earth Day | First observed 1970 |
| 1 May | International Labour Day / Maharashtra & Gujarat Day | Bombay division, 1960 |
| 11 May | National Technology Day | Pokhran-II tests, 1998 |
| 21 May | Anti-Terrorism Day | Rajiv Gandhi's assassination anniversary |
| 31 May | World No-Tobacco Day | WHO |
| 5 June | World Environment Day | Stockholm Conference, 1972 (first observed 1974) |
| 21 June | International Day of Yoga | First observed 2015; proposed by India at the UN |
| 26 June | International Day Against Drug Abuse | UN, 1987 |

Two anchors here are uniquely Indian: **National Technology Day**
commemorates the Pokhran-II nuclear tests of **11 May 1998**, and
the **International Day of Yoga** was proposed by India and adopted
by the UN General Assembly in December 2014, first observed on **21
June 2015**. Both are frequent one-mark questions, and both have
traps: yoga day is *not* a UN "founding" date but an Indian-proposed
observance; technology day is *not* the 1974 "Smiling Buddha" test —
that date (18 May 1974) is a separate fact.

## 3. The Autumn Cluster — July to September

| Date | Observance | The anchor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 July | National Doctors' Day | Dr. B.C. Roy's birthday |
| 11 July | World Population Day | "Day of Five Billion," 1987 |
| 9 August | Quit India Movement anniversary; World Tribal Day (Int'l Day of the World's Indigenous Peoples) | 1942 "Do or Die" |
| 15 August | Independence Day | 1947 |
| 29 August | National Sports Day | Dhyan Chand's birthday (1905) |
| 5 September | Teachers' Day | Dr. S. Radhakrishnan's birthday |
| 8 September | International Literacy Day | UNESCO, 1966 |
| 14 September | Hindi Diwas | Hindi adopted as official language, 1949 |
| 15 September | Engineers' Day (India) | M. Visvesvaraya's birthday |
| 21 September | International Day of Peace | UN |
| 27 September | World Tourism Day | UNWTO |

The 29 August / 5 September pair is the paper's favourite
confusion: **Dhyan Chand for Sports Day, Radhakrishnan for Teachers'
Day**. The trap works because both are teacher-and-sports adjacent
in coaching lists. Hindi Diwas (14 September 1949, Constituent
Assembly adopting Hindi in the Devanagari script as the official
language — via Article 343 of the Constitution that came into force
in 1950) is paired with **English being associate official via
Article 343(2) for 15 years**, extended by the Official Languages
Act, 1963.

## 4. The Year-End Cluster — October to December

| Date | Observance | The anchor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2 October | Gandhi Jayanti; International Day of Non-Violence | UN, 2007 |
| 8 October | Indian Air Force Day | IAF established, 1932 |
| 16 October | World Food Day | FAO founded, 1945 |
| 24 October | United Nations Day | UN Charter in force, 1945 |
| 31 October | National Unity Day (Rashtriya Ekta Diwas) | Sardar Patel's birthday |
| 14 November | Children's Day | Jawaharlal Nehru's birthday |
| 26 November | Constitution Day (Samvidhan Divas) | Constitution adopted, 1949 |
| 1 December | World AIDS Day | First observed 1988 |
| 4 December | Navy Day | Operation Trident, 1971 |
| 10 December | Human Rights Day | UDHR adopted, 1948 |
| 16 December | Vijay Diwas | 1971 war victory |
| 22 December | National Mathematics Day | Srinivasa Ramanujan's birthday (1887) |

The 26 November / 26 January split is the year's most reliable
trap: the Constitution was **adopted on 26 November 1949** and
**came into force on 26 January 1950**. Constitution Day
(Samvidhan Divas) has been observed since 2015 on the adoption
date; Republic Day marks the commencement date. Human Rights Day
(10 December) marks the **Universal Declaration of Human Rights**
in 1948 — a declaration, not a treaty, which is exactly the
distinction the harder papers test.

[example]
A paper asks: "National Mathematics Day is celebrated on the birth
anniversary of — (a) C.V. Raman, (b) Srinivasa Ramanujan, (c) Aryabhata,
(d) Brahmagupta." The December date (22 December) plus the
mathematics label point to Ramanujan (born 1887). The sister trap:
"National Science Day commemorates —" with Ramanujan as an option;
the answer is C.V. Raman's *Raman effect* (28 February). Both days
honour a "Raman," which is precisely why the examiner chose them.
[/example]

[summary]
Anchor-day pairs to lock in: Youth Day–Vivekananda (12 Jan), Army
Day–Cariappa (15 Jan), Science Day–Raman effect (28 Feb), Health
Day–WHO founding (7 Apr), Technology Day–Pokhran-II (11 May),
Environment Day–Stockholm (5 Jun), Yoga Day–UN 2015 (21 Jun),
Doctors' Day–B.C. Roy (1 Jul), Quit India (9 Aug), Sports
Day–Dhyan Chand (29 Aug), Teachers' Day–Radhakrishnan (5 Sep),
Hindi Diwas (14 Sep), Engineers' Day–Visvesvaraya (15 Sep), UN Day
(24 Oct), Unity Day–Patel (31 Oct), Children's Day–Nehru (14 Nov),
Constitution Day–adoption 1949 (26 Nov), Human Rights
Day–UDHR 1948 (10 Dec), Vijay Diwas–1971 (16 Dec), Mathematics
Day–Ramanujan (22 Dec). Where two days share a month-end or a
"Raman," expect the paper to swap them.
[/summary]
